AB  - Mangrove restoration is an effective management strategy for addressing climate change, with significant potential to enhance ecosystem services, particularly carbon sequestration. However, a critical gap remains in understanding the influence of mangrove tree species diversity on carbon storage. Through a meta-analysis of 404 sample sites across 21 countries, we examined the relationship between tree species diversity and carbon storage, emphasizing the roles of complementarity and selection effects. Our findings indicate that mixed-species mangroves surpass single-species forests in enhancing carbon stocks (weighted effect size (RR') = 33.2 % vs. 9.4 %). Tree species diversity promoted carbon sequestration through complementarity effects (0.77, R2 = 0.81), whereas single-species forests mainly depended on selection effects (0.65, R2 = 0.64). The influence of complementarity and selection effects on carbon storage intensifies and stabilizes 7-20 years after restoration. Additionally, precipitation levels below 500 mm negatively affect carbon storage. We recommend prioritizing multi-species communities in mangrove restoration projects to ensure long-term productivity and ecological resilience.
