AB  - This paper introduces GCAM-CDR 1.0, an integratedassessment model for climate policy based on the open-source Global ChangeAnalysis Model (GCAM). GCAM-CDR extends GCAM v5.4 by enabling users to modeladditional carbon dioxide removal (CDR) technologies and additional policiesand controls related to CDR. New CDR technologies include terrestrialenhanced weathering with basalt, ocean liming, and additional versions ofdirect air capture. New CDR policies and controls include integration ofbioenergy with carbon capture and storage (BECCS) into the CDR market,interregional trade in CDR, exogenous control over the rate of growth ofCDR, the ability to set independent targets for emissions abatement and CDR,and a variety of mechanisms for setting demand for CDR at the regionaland/or global level. These extensions enhance users' ability to study thepotential roles of CDR in climate policy.
