AB  - Ocean island basalts (OIB) can host low-temperature carbonates in veins or vesicles. This observation suggests that, in addition to being a potential source for atmospheric CO2 during periods of volcanic activity, OIB can also be a CO2 sink through carbonate precipitation during low-temperature alteration of basalt. The age as well as the origin of low-temperature carbonates in OIB, however, are often not well constrained. In this study, we present carbonate U-Pb ages on a suite of eight OIB samples with variable ages (9.7 to 74.6 Ma) from three different hotspots: Macdonald hotspot (Mangaia Island), Arago-Rurutu hotspot (Tuvalu Islands), and the St. Helena hot-spot (St. Helena Island). We find that vesicle carbonates are up to 2.3 Myr younger (except for a vein carbonate from one sample that is 8.0 Myr younger) than the corresponding 40Ar/39Ar eruption ages of the host lavas, indicating early carbonation of OIB samples. Secondly, we identify and characterize carbonates in three relatively young (0.31 +/- 0.15 Ma to 1.64 +/- 1.14 Ma) OIB samples from the Samoa (Ofu Island), Caroline (Kosrae Island), and Rarotonga (Rarotonga Island) hotspots. While too young for carbonate U-Pb dating, the presence of carbonate in these young OIB samples supports the hypothesis for early formation of carbonates in OIB. In determining the origin of the carbonates, OIB-hosted carbonates have less radiogenic 87Sr/86Sr than seawater at the time of carbonate formation. Carbonate 87Sr/86Sr is similar to the silicate portion of the host basalt in some samples, but higher than the silicate portion of the basalt in others, consistent with incorporation of variable mixtures of mantle-derived and seawater-derived Sr. Combining Sr isotopes with common 207Pb/206Pb ratios (calculated from the carbonate U-Pb isochrons) obtained on low-temperature carbonates in oceanic basalts from this and previous studies show that carbonate 207Pb/206Pb is also similar to, or higher than, the host rock. The delta 18OSMOW data on a subset of the OIB-hosted carbonates are consistent with carbonate precipitation in equilibrium with low-temperature fluids. Finally, trace element concentrations and ratios in the OIBhosted carbonates reported here are indistinguishable from the previously-published low-temperature carbonates from the oceanic (basaltic) crust, consistent with formation mediated by low-temperature fluids. Using this set of geochemical tools for evaluating carbonate genesis, we show that carbonates previously hosted in submarine basalts from the South China Sea (SCS) are indistinguishable from low-temperature carbonates identified in oceanic basalts elsewhere. This observation challenges the original interpretation that SCS carbonates have a magmatic origin.
