AB  - This research investigates the potential of leveraging large-scale seaweed farming to address ecological and economic challenges in Indian coastal waters, focusing on mitigating Harmful Algal Blooms (HABs) and enhancing coastal sustainability. By exploring the nutrient absorption capacity of Kappaphycus alvarezii and Gracilaria edulis, the study demonstrates the potential to reduce nitrogen and phosphorus concentrations by 47,144 tonnes annually, achieving a 20-30 % reduction in HAB prevalence in high-risk zones such as the Gulf of Mannar. The economic analysis highlights annual revenue potential of INR 82.71 billion from seaweed biomass and secondary industries, complemented by carbon credit earnings from sequestering 57.64 metric tonnes of CO2 per hectare. Moreover, biohydrogen production from seaweed biomass, estimated at 56,025 tonnes/year, adds an innovative energy solution valued at INR 8.4 billion annually. The study introduces an Integrated Seaweed Farming-HAB Management Model (ISF-HMM) to guide sustainable policy frameworks. The findings emphasize seaweed farming's dual role in fostering biodiversity, supporting livelihoods, and contributing to climate resilience and renewable energy goals, positioning it as a cornerstone for sustainable coastal management.
