AB  - Mangrove deforestation due to land-use conversions poses a significant threat to the conservation of coastal ecosystems. This study aims to quantify carbon stocks in planted and natural mangroves and forecast future changes in mangrove ecosystem services in Bapatla district, Andhra Pradesh, India. The mean carbon stocks in biomass and soil (top 1 m) of mangroves in Bapatla were found to be 39.8 +/- 25.6 and 122.86 +/- 67.2 C ha- 1, respectively. The InVEST Coastal Blue Carbon model was applied to assess spatio-temporal changes in blue carbon stocks and net sequestration potential in mangrove forests from 1993 to 2050. The economic value of blue carbon ecosystem services in Bapatla mangroves was evaluated by analysing the changes in carbon storage over 57 years. Future projections from the InVEST model indicate a net increase in carbon sequestration (0.83 Mt C from 2022) under the business-as-usual scenario. The net carbon sequestration between 1993 and 2050 is projected to be roughly 1.42 Mt C over this period. The monetary value of blue carbon ecosystem services (regulatory) for the entire mangrove area of the region ranged from gains of US$300,000 to 1550,000 per year and losses of US$40,000 to 206,666 per year, based on recent estimates of the social cost of carbon (SCC). The study further revealed that due to sea level rise under the high emissions scenario (RCP 8.5), there could be a significant loss of mangrove area and enhanced CO2 emissions by 2100 in this coastal landscape. These findings on the potential changes in land use carbon stocks, sequestration and corresponding regulatory services would inform the development of public policies that restore degraded mangrove habitats, engage local communities in conservation efforts and support existing mangrove protection laws in the Krishna Delta region to effectively mitigate the impacts of climate change.
