AB  - This review examines how seagrasses shape coastal systems through key ecosystem services, including sediment dynamics, wave energy dissipation, and carbon sequestration. Field studies report wave attenuation across meadows of 2.5-70% relative to unvegetated beds. Absolute values of empirical drag exponents, expressed as a function of the Reynolds number, range from 1.45 to 4 depending on wave height and context, while drag coefficients related to the Keulegan-Carpenter number yield values of 1.1-2.7. Wave decay across meadow extents is captured by decay exponents of 0.004-0.04. Accretion rates typically range from 0.13 to 4 mm yr(-1), with reported values reaching 9.9 mm yr(-1), and seagrasses are shown to affect not only colonized areas but also adjacent habitats, highlighting the importance of broader ecosystem impacts, long-term eco-geomorphic trajectories, and connectivity. Carbon sequestration is widely recognised, though estimates vary substantially due to differences in plant traits, soil and environmental conditions, complicating quantifications and scaling. Degradation of seagrass stocks could release similar to 1154 Tg CO2 (665-1699 Tg), representing a social cost of similar to$213 billion (2020 USD). Overall, seagrass-mediated processes are highly sensitive to local drivers, including hydrodynamics, spatial heterogeneity, and anthropogenic pressures, underscoring the need for robust links between site-specific descriptors and ecosystem functions. Such understanding is critical for evaluating regulating services and guiding restoration and coastal protection strategies.
