AB  - The growing global demand for freshwater along with the environmental burden of conventional desalination technologies, highlights the need for cleaner and economically viable alternatives. Biodesalination, carrying out by aquatic microalgae, offers a promising route for converting saline or brackish water into usable freshwater while minimizing ecological impact. This review examines the biological mechanisms that govern salt reduction through bioadsorption and bioaccumulation and emphasizes halotolerant species such as Dunaliella and Scenedesmus known for high desalination efficiency. In natural and engineered set up, microalgae-bacteria consortia further enhance performance through organic compound degradation, nutrient recycling, and ionic stabilization, leading to enhanced biomass and salt-reduction efficiency. In addition, microalgae provide a dual benefit by capturing atmospheric CO2 through photosynthetic bio-fixation, thereby linking water reclamation with climate-mitigation potential. Operational parameters such as pH, temperature and light intensity, as well as the use of photobioreactor systems, are discoursed for process optimization. In spite of encouraging prospects, large-scale deployment remains challenging because of energy requirements and economic constraints. Future research ought to focus on genetic engineering new tools to improve salt tolerance and CO2 fixation, hybrid systems integrating biological and conventional desalination, and wastewater recycling as a nutrient source. Thus, microalgae assisted bio-desalination signifies a sustainable, multi-benefit platform to address global water scarcity and environmental degradation.
