AB  - Coastal wetland sediments are significant carbon sinks. Quantifying sediment carbon stocks and identifying factors controlling them across ecosystem types are essential for guiding effective restoration strategies. In this study, we compared sediment organic carbon (SOC) stocks (to 1 m depth) between salt marshes and bare flats Nanhui Shoal, China, and examined the influence of organic carbon (OC) sources and sediment properties SOC storage. Relative to bare flats, salt marsh sediments were finer grained, had significantly higher OC content, and exhibited lower bulk density. Consequently, salt marshes, characterized by multiple OC sources and sediment properties conducive to OC stabilization, had higher SOC stocks (to 1 m depth). Specifically, SOC stocks 1 m depth) in salt marshes at Nanhui Shoal were approximately 40% higher per unit area than those in bare flats. These results suggest that, following the removal of the invasive species Spartina alterniflora, assisted restoration should be implemented to accelerate the recovery of native vegetation, such as Phragmites australis. This approach will help sustain SOC stocks in coastal wetlands and enhance sediment stabilization, coastal protection, and biodiversity conservation.
