AB  - Accurate estimation of seaweed biomass plays a crucial role in the development of marine ranching, seaweed aquaculture, and marine conservation. This study focuses on Sargassum henslowianum, a dominant macroalga in the coastal waters of Nan'ao Island, Guangdong Province. Using a whole plant harvesting approach, we developed biomass models for individual thalli, incorporating morphological traits such as plant height, branch number, holdfast diameter, and canopy width as single or multiple independent variables. The biomass of different structural components, including leaves, holdfasts, receptacles, stipes, air bladders, and whole plants, was treated as the dependent variable. Additionally, Standardized Major Axis Estimation (SMA) was applied to examine the allometric growth relationships among these structures, and an elemental analyzer was used to quantify their carbon content. The results revealed substantial variation in morphological traits and biomass allocation during the late reproductive stage of S. henslowianum. Stipes accounted for the highest proportion of biomass (51%), followed by leaves (27%), whereas air bladders had the lowest allocation (3%). Biomass models for both individual organs and whole plants followed a power law function, with plant height & times; canopy width (HD) identified as the optimal predictor. The best fit biomass model for whole plants was y = 0.004(HD)1.02 (R2 = 0.720). Allometric analysis indicated that most organ to organ and organ to whole plant relationships deviated from isometric scaling, except for holdfast stipe, holdfast whole plant, leaf stipe, and stipe whole plant relationships, which approximated an isometric growth pattern. Among all structures, receptacles exhibited the fastest biomass accumulation, while holdfasts had the highest total carbon content. By establishing biomass models and elucidating organ level growth relationships, this study provides a robust framework for predicting the biomass and carbon storage of S. henslowianum, offering insights into its growth strategies and resource allocation. Furthermore, these findings contribute to the methodological advancement of biomass modeling and carbon stock estimation for other large macroalgae.
