AB  - Litterfall and decomposition provide a significant contribution to energy and nutrient cycles in the mangrove ecosystems. However, what remains unclear is how small-scale mangrove forests contribute their litterfall to the carbon pool and the enhancement of ecosystem services with changing water systems. Hence, the present study was conducted to assess the leaf litter decomposition of Lumnitzera racemosa in a temporarily waterlogged (site A), permanently waterlogged, (site B) and non-waterlogged location (site C), of the Kadol Kale mangrove forest, Sri Lanka and understand the social cost of carbon (SCC) of litter production of this tropical mangrove forest. The study consisted of litterfall sampling and leaf litter decomposition analysis conducted in 3 study sites during rainy and dry periods of 2019 considering 21, 42, 63, 84 and 137 days of sampling. There was no significant difference in the decomposition rate of three selected sites in the selected days. The leaf litter decomposition of L. racemosa showed a three-phase pattern with an initial slow phase, intermediate fast phase and a final slow phase. However, mass loss showed differences at sites during the decomposition process. The trend of organic carbon concentration in leaf litter showed a rapid increase during the initial phase and gradually decreased in all sites till the end of experiment while the nitrogen concentration of the leaf litter of L. racemosa gradually increased significantly during the experiment period. The variations in the C:N ratio of the leaf litter during the decomposition phases indicated that L. racemosa leaf litter can significantly contribute to the biogeochemical cycles of the mangrove ecosystem by adding nutrients which may in turn contribute to soil fertility. The highest leaf litter carbon concentration per unit area (2.56 kg/ha) with highest mean decomposition rate (0.04 +/- 0.01), decay constant (0.018/day), and half-life ((t(50)) = 38.5 days) was observed in site C claiming that non-flooded conditions of mangroves have favored more carbon storing than flooded and temporally flooded conditions. Further, the SCC for carbon sequestration through litter production of Kadol Kale mangrove forest was quantified as US$4.10 per tonne of CO2. By quantifying the economic value of CO2 sequestration through litter decomposition, this study demonstrates the contribution of Kadol Kale mangrove forest to climate-change mitigation and strengthens the economic justification for protecting and restoring mangrove ecosystems.
